# Normalise line endings so a Windows checkout and a Linux CI checkout hold the
# SAME bytes.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. With `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) and no
# .gitattributes, Git rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. Roughly a
# dozen tests in src/test/ are SOURCE SCANNERS: they read a .js/.jsx/.ts file and
# assert on its contents, splitting on '\n' and anchoring regexes with '$'. A
# trailing '\r' breaks every one of those anchors, so those tests fail on a
# Windows working tree and pass in CI - the worst kind of failure, because the
# machine that can reproduce it is not the machine that reports it.
#
# Measured on 2026-08-24: src/components/VehicleTyreDiagram.jsx was checked out
# with 1,644 CRLF line endings and 0 bare LF, and the layout scanner in
# src/test/vehicleTyreLayout.test.js found 0 layouts. With '\r' stripped it found
# all 13.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the WORKING TREE to LF on every platform. The repository itself
# already stores LF (autocrlf converts on commit), so this changes no committed
# content - only what lands on disk at checkout.
#
# The source scanners were ALSO made line-ending agnostic, so they no longer
# depend on this file being respected. Both halves are deliberate: this stops the
# whole class, the per-test normalisation stops it in a tree checked out before
# this file existed.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be touched.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.pptx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks   binary
*.p12   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary

# Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF to run.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
